  "worked_solution": "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5x).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5x}\\cdot 5=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e/5$ | ${e}^{4}/5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e/5}^{{e}^{4}/5}\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{e/5}^{{e}^{4}/5}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20614-\\operatorname{ln}\u20611 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20614\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
